  "root_cause_analysis": {
    "why_baseline_3_frames_failed": "In baseline frame_01, frame_02, and frame_04, the smartphone is resting flat on a black armchair armrest/cushion. The object bounding box (~100,000 px^2) is dominated by the structural features, dark foam, and armrest curvature of the office chair. YOLO11n outputs class 'chair' with confidence 0.709 (IoU=0.826) instead of 'cell phone'. This is a visual context dominance phenomenon, not an inherent inability of YOLO11n to detect phones.",
    "why_handheld_phones_succeed": "When a phone is held in hand (vertical near chest/face, horizontal typing, angled) or placed flat on an uncluttered tabletop, YOLO11n readily detects the phone with confidence scores reaching 0.56 to 0.88 and high spatial precision (IoU up to 0.77). Across all 30 visible surveillance frames, handheld and tabletop phone recall reaches 80.0% to 100.0% depending on angle.",
    "why_high_res_upscaling_degrades_recall": "Upscaling from 640 to 1280 pixels increases inference latency by 3.6x (from 79.5ms to 289ms, dropping FPS from 12.6 to 3.5 FPS) while paradoxically degrading phone detection recall. YOLO11n anchors and convolutional feature pyramids are calibrated for 640x640. At 1280px, receptive fields expand, causing small objects to be split across fine-grained cells without sufficient contextual pooling.",
    "animal_gate_isolation": "Real dogs activate dog class features with confidence >0.88, while rectangular dark phones held in hand produce low-level gradient noise that only rarely triggers animal feature maps at confidence <0.09. By locking the animal class gate to 0.55, phone edge artifacts are 100% eliminated (0 false positives) without suppressing genuine dogs."
  }
